   "text": "This is my reading of the drawing, written as SMILES. The stereodescriptors were not derived centre by centre from the wedges. I took them from the natural rifamycin configuration, which I recalled from memory and could not fully cross-check against the image, so they are the least certain part.\n\nThe structure is a rifamycin core fused to a phenoxazine ring system. I read it region by region:\n\n- **Ansa chain:**\n  - The enol ether O\u2013CH=CH leads to CH(OMe), then CH(CH3), then CH(OAc), then CH(CH3).\n  - Next come CH(OH), CH(CH3), CH(OH) and CH(CH3), then a CH=CH\u2013CH=C(CH3) diene.\n  - The diene ends in an amide C(=O)\u2013NH attached to the aromatic core.\n- **Ketal and furanone:** the quaternary carbon carries a methyl and is bonded to the enol-ether oxygen and the ring oxygen. It sits next to a C=O on the fused furanone ring.\n- **Naphthoquinone part:** an aromatic ring bears a methyl and an OH. It is fused to a second ring that has a C=O and the C\u2013NH carbon.\n- **Phenoxazine part:** the ring N= and O bridge the naphthoquinone to a benzene ring. That benzene carries an OH next to the N-bearing carbon and an O\u2013CH2CH2\u2013N(CH3)\u2013C(=O)CH3 side chain.\n\nSMILES: CO[C@H]1/C=C/O[C@@]2(C)Oc3c(C)c(O)c4c(c3C2=O)c3nc5c(O)cc(OCCN(C)C(C)=O)cc5oc3c(NC(=O)/C(C)=C\\C=C\\[C@H](C)[C@@H](O)[C@@H](C)[C@H](O)[C@H](C)[C@H](OC(C)=O)[C@@H]1C)c4=O"
